    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 112310\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n410 PM PDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Widespread scattered shower activity will continue through\n  Monday, with elevated flash flood risk continuing for Clark, \n  Lincoln, and Mohave counties through tonight.\n\n* Gusty south winds continue through the day today, gradually \n  tapering off through Monday.\n\n* Below-normal temperatures expected, with portions of the \n  southwestern Great Basin freezing early in the work week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Saturday.\n\nWidespread heavy rainfall this morning resulted in flood impacts \nacross portions of southeastern California, southern Nevada, and \nnorthwestern Arizona with peak rainfall measurements ranging from \n2.50 to over 3.00 inches. The center of the remnants of Rachel \nwill continue to push north-northeastward through the day today, \nbringing persistent concerns for flash flooding across Clark \nCounty, Lincoln County, and Mohave County through late tonight. \nMeanwhile, Inyo, San Bernardino, Esmeralda, and central Nye \ncounties have been removed from the Flood Watch. While isolated-\nto-scattered showers remain possible in these areas, the \nwidespread concern for flash flooding has subsided. A longwave \ntrough will approach the region Monday, with its associated trough\naxis swinging through the Desert Southwest Monday night. As a \nresult, a resurgence of precipitation chances return Monday \nevening, though rain rates are expected to be low-to-moderate. \nChances of rain will decrease through the day on Tuesday.\n\nGusty south winds blew through the region this morning, with\nseveral sites across Mohave and Lincoln counties observing 55-65\nmph winds associated with the main circulation of the remnants of\nRachel. Wind gust speeds have decreased through the day today, so\nthe High Wind Warning has been downgraded to a Wind Advisory \nthrough 5 AM Monday morning. Expect persistent southerly winds \nacross the forecast area between 30 and 45 mph today. On Monday, \ngusts will continue, but will range between 20 and 30 mph.\n\nA cold front associated with the aforementioned upper-level trough \nwill push through the Desert Southwest Monday night, bringing much \ncooler temperatures to the region. A Freeze Watch is in effect for \nthe northern Owens Valley, Esmeralda County, and central Nye County, \ndue to the expectation of the first freeze of the season Tuesday \nmorning. For the remainder of the region, temperatures are expected \nto remain above-freezing, but will range 15-20 degrees below-\nnormal... equating to forecast high temperatures in Las Vegas in the \nupper-60s on Tuesday. Temperatures gradually recover through the \nwork week into next weekend as a heights rise in the Desert \nSouthwest.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Gusty \nsoutherly to southwesterly winds will continue this evening, with \ngusts to around 25KT expected through around midnight when winds \ndiminish. Lingering showers will persist early in the forecast \nperiod, especially along the Mormon Mesa and Peach Springs \nCorridors, with dry conditions overnight, and a return of showers \nmid-morning through the afternoon. There is low confidence that \nany showers or thunderstorms directly impact the terminal this \nafternoon or Monday afternoon, though erratic gusty winds could \nresult from showers or storms nearby. Outside of convective \ninfluences, southwesterly winds with gusts to around 25KT return \nby early Monday afternoon, continuing through early evening. \nCeilings will generally remain SCT-BKN between 8-10kft, lower in \nthe vicinity of any showers. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Showers continue over\nnorthwestern Arizona and the southern Great Basin early in the \nperiod, with activity expected to gradually diminish through the \nevening, with mostly dry conditions overnight. Additional showers \nwill develop early morning along the Lower Colorado River Valley \nand in northwestern Arizona, gradually expanding northward through\nthe day. Outside of influences from showers or storms, southerly \nto southwesterly winds with gusts to 20- 30KT will continue this \nevening, diminishing overnight, and returning by late \nmorning/early afternoon. Ceilings will generally remain between \n8-12kft, lower in the vicinity of showers, with potential for \nheavier rain to reduce visibility. In the northern Owens \nValley/KBIH, winds shift to the northwest late in the forecast \nperiod, with gusts to around 20KT.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report \nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
